Moving from InMemoryUserDetailsManager to a Database (like H2 or MySQL) is like moving from a handwritten list on a sticky note to a digital filing cabinet.
When you make this shift, the "Manager" changes, the "Storage" changes, and most importantly, your Signup flow finally starts working for real.
1. The Core Change: The UserDetailsService
In the "In-Memory" version, Spring uses a built-in class. When you move to a DB, you usually write your own implementation of UserDetailsService.
| Feature | InMemoryUserDetailsManager | Database (JPA/Hibernate) |
|---|---|---|
| Logic | Loops through a Java List or Map. | Executes a SQL query: SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? |
| Storage | Hardcoded in your Java Config. | Stored on a disk or in-memory tables (H2). |
| Runtime Signup | Impossible (Hardcoded). | Possible. You can save() new users anytime. |
2. The 3 New Components You Need
To move to a DB, you have to add these three layers to your project:
A. The Entity (The Table Blueprint)
You create a class that represents your table.
@Entity
public class UserEntity {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String username;
    private String password;
    private String roles; // e.g., "ROLE_USER,ROLE_ADMIN"
}

B. The Repository (The Data Access)
You create an interface. You don't even have to write the SQL; Spring Data JPA does it for you!
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<UserEntity, Long> {
    Optional<UserEntity> findByUsername(String username);
}

C. The Custom Service (The Bridge)
This is what the JWT Filter calls. It uses the Repository to look into the DB.
@Service
public class CustomUserDetailsService implements UserDetailsService {
    @Autowired private UserRepository repository;

    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) {
        UserEntity user = repository.findByUsername(username)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException("User not found!"));
        
        return new User(user.getUsername(), user.getPassword(), new ArrayList<>());
    }
}

3. How the "Signup" Flow changes
This is the biggest win.
 * With In-Memory: When a user hits /signup, you have nowhere to put them. You can't "edit" your @Bean code while the app is running.
 * With DB: Your SignupController simply calls userRepository.save(newUser).
   * The user is written to the H2/MySQL table.
   * Now, when that user tries to Login, the CustomUserDetailsService will find them in the database.
4. How the "Filter" behaves differently
Actually, for your JwtAuthenticationFilter, nothing changes! This is the beauty of Spring Security’s architecture. The filter just says: "Hey, UserDetailsService, give me the details for 'Adesh'." * It doesn't care if the service gets those details from a Hardcoded List, a Database, or even an external API call.
 * As long as it gets a UserDetails object back, the filter is happy.
Summary Checklist for moving to DB:
 * Add Dependencies: Add spring-boot-starter-data-jpa and your DB driver (H2/MySQL).
 * Define Properties: Set your DB URL, username, and password in application.properties.
 * Create Entity & Repository: Map your Java code to the DB table.
 * Remove the InMemory Bean: Delete the InMemoryUserDetailsManager bean from your config.
 * Inject your Custom Service: Spring will automatically find your CustomUserDetailsService and use it.
